>I have several DecStation 3100's, and would like to attach modems without
>spending outrageous amounts of money on DEC modem cables (I have lots of
>spare parts to make my own cables, just no MMJ plugs or DEC MMJ cables to
>examine).

	You can purchase MMJ plugs/sockets and cable from third party outfits
like BlackBox or Digi-Key.  Digital part numbers are

	MMJ Plug = H8220-00 (Quan 50) for stranded wire
                   H8226-00 (Quan 50) for solid wire
	Crimp tool = H8421
        6 wire 26 AWG stranded data cable = H8240-00 (1000 ft spool)
	MMJ to EIA-232 Male adapter = H8571-A

>
>Does someone have a pinout for the MMJ serial ports on the back?
>
	Here you go
   
           -----------------
           |  | | | | | |  |
           |  1 2 3 4 5 6  |
           |_________      |
                     | Tab |           
                     |_____|


	1= Ready Out (DTR)
	2= TX+
	3= TX- (gnd)
        4= RX-
        5= RX+
        6= Ready In (DSR)



>Also, can anyone tell me if
>the serial "printer" port is just another full
>function serial port? Is there any reason I cannot use it for another
>modem or direct serial link to a PC perhaps?

	It is just another serial line, /dev/tty03 to ULTRIX.

As to whether of not it is a "full function serial port", you can see by the
diagram above that there is NO support for signals like RSLD  (carrier detect),
CTS, and RTS so I wouldn't characterize either DECconnect port as a "full
function" serial port. In fact there is a real hack inside all VAXstations and
DECstations that used the six pin MMJ. The serial I/O section uses a chipset based on the OLD DZ11 Unibus
Communications Multiplexor. When the designers hooked up the I/O signals from
DECconnect ports to the chipset they left the unused inputs for CTS and RLSD
on the chip unconnected, not even pulled up or down. Fortunately the chip is
forgiving and assumes the input = low for this condition. Unfortunately this
means that RLSD and CTS are ALWAYS de-asserted. What this means is that the
OS cann't treat these ports as "modem" ports because when it tries to the
signals necessary to support "full modem control" aren't complete and the 
device driver fails to see these signals ever assert so it keeps cycling DTR
to the port trying to clear what it believes is an error condition, The OS
drivers are designed to support the ITU-T V.25, V.25bis, and V.nn modem signaling
protocols where when DSR=true, RLSD and CTS must become true within 30 seconds
or and error condition is declared and DTR is de-asserted.

	As a direct link to a PC using DTR/DSR to indicate device ready these
ports should work fine. Maximum bit rate supported is 19,200 bit/s.

	\Bob Lail
