osyjm@cs.montana.edu (Jaye Mathisen) writes:

>I am not impressed with DEC's use of a RAID controller, rather than
>using stock scsi and doing the RAID internally.  That said, I would
>like to get a DEC RAID box to use on an LPX 560 with Intel NT.  However,
>it doesn't look good.  THe last storageworks catalog only mentions
>Alpha NT. (So much for code portability).

Well, you could use the StorageWorks box on NT-AS simply by using
an Adaptec SCSI controller and enabling the RAID-5 striped parity
set in the NT-AS fault tolerance subsystem.  As a matter of fact,
standard NT also can do RAID-5 with an undocumented hack (it was
something simple like telling the fault tolerance program that
you are running WFWG).  Standard NT _or_ NT-AS can operate as 
a RAID-1 (mirrored) array of drives.

In these configurations, the DEC box is not doing much for you
that any other OEM RAID enclosure, such as the ones we make,
couldn't do.  One issue of interest is in enclosure environmental
reporting -- the Storage Works box uses a proprietary handshaking
interface to report on fan or power supply failure.  I suppose one
could write an NT/NT-AS driver that hooks these lines to a parallel
port on the PC, but Digital is loath to release the protocol for
their system, but they could write the driver if there was sufficient
customer interest.  (Our box does this function via a RS-232 serial
port; the protocol is available to anyone interested).      

Work will soon be underway in one of the SCSI-3 subcommittees to
standardize a SCSI reporting mechanism for enclosure information,
and for control of things like error LEDs etc.

>Alternately, I'm looking for low cost RAID (RAIDION?) w/o all the 
>bells and whistles, but basic RAID 0,1,0+1,3,5 in some combination. (not
>all required, 0+1, 3, or 5 is what I'm looking at.  (I realize 0+1
>kind of wipes out the low-cost aspect).
>Any tips appreciated.  Part numbers even more so.  Salesmen not wanted.

CMD Technologies and Mylex both offer SCSI-to-SCSI RAID bridge 
controllers, with varying prices and functionality.  Mylex
also makes a RAID controller that fits in a PCI slot on the 
motherboard, but I don't know what their status is on NT/NT-AS
but I assume they either do have it or soon will.  Both CMD 
and Mylex are extremely high performance devices which cost
in the $3K to $6K price range, depending upon configuration.


