In article <15SEP199422234878@ctrvx1.vanderbilt.edu>, critchss@ctrvx1.vanderbilt.edu (Steven Critchfield) writes:
> I have a tu78 that I cannot seem to get the tapes to load correctly. The one
> time it seemed to be loading correctly, it ran all the tape off of the case and 
> onto its own spool without leaving any to let it rewind. I have 30 tapes to
> switch between.

  The TU77/8/9 drives are wonderful drives, but rather picky to adjust 
properly. [BTW, the current owner of the company that made them is
Triumph/Adler].

  There are a *large* number of adjustments and if you fiddle with one of
them, you generally need to re-adjust all the others.

  I assume you're getting a "load fault" LED (probably accompanied by some
major foulup in the vacuum columns) when trying to load a new, 2400' tape.
If you're trying to load a short (600', 1200', etc.) tape, get 2400' working
first. Likewise, if you're trying to autoload tapes in IBM "Easyload II"
canisters, give up. There's an FCO for it and even then it's iffy. Also, make
sure the tape has the end correctly crimped and stiffened (there's a tool for
this, comes with each drive).

  Rather than telling you what adjusts what, let me describe the whole system
in general terms. First, you've got several air valves and pressure sensors
(these are on/off type sensors) with both pressure and vacuum. As the load
starts, the air will blow the tape's leading end up into the top of the load
chute, where vacuum will pull it in. One set of adjustments is for the speed
that the upper tape reel unwinds the tape. Once the tape comes out the bottom
of the load chute, the air will blow it onto the lower reel. There's another
speed adjustment for the lower reel. There's vacuum on the hub of the lower
reel to "stick" the tape to the reel. There's an FCO for the air supply on
the bottom as well. If the reels turn too fast/slow, you'll get too much/not
enough tape. The ratio needs to be right, and the actual speed also has limits.
Any air pressure sensor can foul this part of the loading up. Another common
problem is the rubber caps on the pressure test points tend to rot and leak.

  Once the tape is on both reels and wound a bit, vacuum comes on and the tape
moves into the columns. Both on/off and variable transducers read the position
of the tape loops in the column (on/off sensors serve as emergency limiters,
while the variable ones set the idle park positions of the loops near the
center of the columns). Trimpots on the Interconnect F1 board (lot's of 'em)
set the centering as well as the "stiffness" of the loops. A problem here
will usually cause the tape to unload in the columns.

  If all is well so far, the drive will advance the tape to BOT. If BOT isn't
detected in something like 30 feet, you'll get a load fault. Problems here
are the BOT/EOT sensor (located in the load chute) or the lower reel index
transducer that counts reel revolutions by reflecting off a gap in the hub.
If this transducer fails, you can also get load problems back at the first
step. If it fails with tape loaded, a rewind won't slow down near BOT and will
instead run off the takeup (lower) reel at high speed.

  As you can see, troubleshooting this drive needs lots of experience in both
mechanical, air-flow, and electronic subsystems. To do it right, you'll also
need vacuum and pressure gauges, a tachometer to measure the reel speed, and
a set of spare parts. Training also helps 8-). I've worked on these drives
for about 8 years now, and they can still surprise me.

