In article <357f1b$20i@jac.zko.dec.com> lionel@quark.enet.dec.com (Steve Lionel) writes:

   In article <357979$lh6@iia.org>, vogelt@iia.org (Tim Vogel) writes:
   |>I recently picked up a Dec Vaxstation 3100.  I am in need of information 
   |>on what it is, if there is a difference between a DECstation and a DEC 
   |>VAXstation, and what the machine might be worth.  The machine has 24 megs 
   |>of RAM, and two 100-meg hard drives, 3.5" floppy, keyboard and mouse.  
   |>The machine itself seems to work, but the monitor I got with it is broken 
   |>(19" DEC monitor).  I can hook the machine to another monitor that I have 
   |>, but the scan rate is too high, so I can only see a little of the booting.
   |>
   |>So what is this machine, and what is it worth?
   |>Is there somewhere I can get a DEC 19" monitor fixed?
   |>Does this machine run VMS or ultrix?  How can I tell easily?

   It would help if you could quote the exact model number from the label on
   the back of the unit.  Digital sold a number of different models of
   "VAXstation 3100", though if that's all yours says on the front, it's
   probably a 3100/40, one of the early models.  VAXstations are different
   from DECstations; the latter have MIPS processors and run RISC ULTRIX.
   VAX ULTRIX is retired - I don't know if it was available for the
   VAXstation 3100.  Otherwise it will run VMS.

I wouldn't call VAX/ULTRIX "retired", because Digital still will sell
and still supports VAX/ULTRIX for those who want it/have it.  ULTRIX
as an entire product is sort-of in "maintenance" mode, meaning that
there probably won't be any new features added, but that customers
will be supported on it in to the indefinite future.

   I would expect that the CPU used in your model is the "CVAX" chipset,
   about 3.5VUPS.  You should be able to attach an ANSI terminal to the serial
   output connector on back and use that as a console.  There's a small slide
   switch somewhere on the back panel which needs to be thrown in order to enable
   the terminal as console.

For the uninitiated, a "VUP" is a VAX Unit of Processing (Power), and
amounts to about a "pure MIPS" (in other words, 1 VUP = 1 million
instructions per second, where each instruction does one useful
complete task).  Figure that 3.5 VUPS is around what you get out
of a 386DX 16MHz PC, very roughly.

Note that the VAXstation series had one, or sometimes two integral
SCSI controllers, and a decent internal ("local") bus, resulting
in their being able to handle a good amount of I/O.  So, don't take
the comparison above to a fairly slow PC to mean that the system
isn't worth anything!

