In article <35c827$c6t@news.acns.nwu.edu>, tward@andre.mech.nwu.edu (Tim Ward) writes:
>
>To clean rrz5g I run fsck, but it complains about invalid information
>in the superblock and says to use an alternate superblock.  Using 
>fsck -b 32 on the partition, fsck proceeds normally, and caches two
>errors one in Phase 4 (bad inode number in superblock), the other in 
>Phase 5 (something about a bad cylinder group).  fsck offers to fix them
>and I accept.  Then when it tries to verify the changes, the error message
>about bad info in the superblock re-appears.  This has happened for two
>alternate superblocks as well (32 and 8720).
>
>Anyone have an idea on how to fsck this disk successfully?

1.  Verify the disk is crashing on you.  Use rzdisk with the -s
    option to scan the disk for bad blocks.  If a scan finds some,
    use the -r option to replace them and run the scan again.
    Repeat until the scan doesn't find any bad blocks.  If it
    takes more than one or two passes don't bother...

2.  Unless that version had a bug in fsck, it will try to rewrite
    the superblock.  V3.anything is pretty old though and I don't
    recall what bugs existed.  One thing to try is verify that
    fsck really did fix the problems.  Run another pass with the
    same backup superblock and see if it really was repaired.

3.  When all else fails, make a physical backup of the partitions
    desired.  You can use dd(1) and a good safe block size is 8 KB.

	dd if=/dev/rrz5g of=/dev/rmt0h bs=8k

    This way you can get the disk working and not worry much about
    the data.  If the problem was the disk, then you can re-create
    the partition table and restore the physical image of the partition
    and then fix it.
