In article <CwE898.3J8@ritz.mordor.com>, stevenb@ritz.mordor.com (Steven Bernstein) writes...
>This request is for a friend with limited Internet capabilities:
> 
>He is interested in reading some diskettes that have been written on a
>Foxboro Video Spec 3 and 4 System (sold in the early 80's).  It used some
>type of compression before the information was written to the disk. The
>compression is most likely "DEC RAD 50" - while I know very little about
>this, my friend is pretty certain.
> 
>Any information concerning this compression or how to read/translate these
>floppies into a vintage 90's (GIF, JPEG format?) would be greatly
>appreciated. 
> 
>Thanks, in advance, for any advice or pointers!  Sorry to make you dig
>up old brain cells on this one. 8^)
> 
>Regards,
> .steve.

RAD50 was a scheme for saving a few bits while storing alphanumeric text,
not for image compression.  If the Foxboro machine was based on a DEC
operating system and DEC hardware, it is very likely that the file names
are in RAD50.  But the images, if compressed at all, would be compressed
with some other scheme.

If you can find more details of the machine the disks were written on,
or if the machine still is in operation, let us know.  If your friend is really
serious about recovering these images, pass my name onto him and we'll
work something out.

