> GED::B12635         pjt@pelab.allied.com      b12635@vaxc.phx1.aro.allied.com

There are two parts to the dictionary definition (Yes, Merriam-Webster, but
others will give much the same):
   a.  An acronym is a WORD.
   b.  An acromym is formed from parts of other words and phrases.
So, now let's look up WORD (in Merriam-Webster):
   1. Something that is said.
   2. A speech sound or series of speech sounds that communicates meaning.
      also: a graphic representation of such a sound or series of sounds.
Thus, for English speakers at least, "IBM" cannot be a word, and hence is not
an acronym, but merely an abbreviation.  On the other hand "DEC" could be (and
usually is) treated as a word, therefore it is an acronym when used as such.

