Keywords: Alpha, profiling tools
X-Newsreader: mxrn 6.18-5


DEC OSF/1 provides several tools for profiling application programs.

1. The standard UN*X prof and gprof tools are available.  These provide
   a statistical PC-sampling profile of your application.  Gprof also
   provides callee-caller information.

2. OSF/1 also supports the pixie tool.  This tools modifies an executable
   image producing a new image that counts each basic block's
   execution.  You then run the new image to produce a profile of the
   application.  The prof postprocessor allows you to display execution
   cycle counts, test coverage statistics, callee-caller information, etc.

3. A new tool, Atom, allows users to build custom analysis tools for
   their applications.  Like pixie, Atom modifies an executable image
   and produces a new image.  However, Atom is much more powerful.  Some
   of the tools that have been built with Atom range from a simple
   procedure entry/exit profiler to cache simulators and tools that
   detect errors with dynamic memory allocation and usage (like Purify).
   You can use Atom to design your own tools or can use any of the tools
   that ship with Atom.

4. Most implementations of the the Alpha architecture support
   performance counters that keep track of various CPU statistics (like
   the number of stall cycles, the number of dual issues, the number of
   first level cache misses, etc.)  OSF/1 provides a tool, uprofile,
   that allows users to measure these events and report where in their
   application they occurred.  A postprocessor allows you to display a
   histogram output of where these events occurred in your application.

If you purchase the FUSE product, it provides GUI's for the prof and
pixie tools as well as for the rest of the development environment.

There are also a host of tools that measure system performance.  I'm not
as familiar with these, but I know things like vmstat and nfsstat are
supported.  There is also a tool, Polycenter, that helps tune the AdvFS
file system.  There is a whole manual, "System Tuning and Performance
Management", that discusses these tools.  If you have access to any DEC
OSF/1 documentation, this might be a good place to look.

I hope this helps answer your questions.

