I've run into a problem and the software vendor is starting to point
fingers. The problem is with an implementation of TCP/IP which I run
on a VAX still on VMS 4.7. The reasons for staying on 4.7 is that its
a microVAX II and the current disk is an RD54, and it does what I need
it to do.

The TCP/IP is designed for VMS 4 right up to OpenVMS 6.X, but The SMTP
mail crashes the system, it started a few weeks ago, and the software 
engineers are saying that the problem may be in VMS.

Specifically, I found that the very same image executing *** E V E R Y *** 
time the system crashes and ANALYZE/IMAGE reports an error with a global 
section, Image section descriptor extends beyond format for global section. 
To compair this to another image, I chose FINGER.EXE which I created from 
the TCP/IP examples directory. To my surprise, it reported the same 
error. I then checked all TCP/IP images on my system. The TCP/IP support
utilities are OK as are the device drivers, but every image which performs
socket connections over TCP/IP links has the very same problem.

The ANALYZE/CRASH reports the crash being caused by an XQPERR, Error 
detected by file system XQP. An XQP is to Structure Level 2 Files-11 
volumes as ACP's are to Structure Level 1 volumes. The XQP's execute 
in kernel mode. I can't confirm this since I don't have access to the 
source code but I'm thinking that the system forces a crash with an XQPERR
bugcheck when the code violates the boundry of the global section while 
in kernel mode.

The software vendor is saying that the error reported by ANALYZE/IMAGE is
a mismatch error between the 4.7 linker and ANALYZE/IMAGE.

Does anyone remember if anything like this has happened in the past?


