                                                 ^^^^no          ^^^^^^^yes

You don't really need to run xmodmap on the VMS side -- just use the
UNIX version already on your Sun.  That will take effect for all
clients connecting to your X11 server.  Of course, all your other
clients must be able to work with the new Backspace/Delete layout.

I am not sure how DECwindows apps that already understand BackSpace
will work when you exchange the two keys.  If they don't like the
switch, you can reprogram them via XmText.translations (or something
like that.  I know I once got something like this working with VAX VMS
DECwrite displaying to an SGI system, but I don't have the exact
resources handy...I can look them up for anybody interested.)

>unix?  I am particularly puzzled because when I start a DECmail window (again
>using startx), and I edit a mail message, my backspace key works fine.

I think the difference is that DECwindows (Motif) can automatically
handle this (and thus the normal apps. like DECwrite, DECmail, etc work
correctly), but DECterm handles the keyboard at a different layer that
doesn't do this remaping for you.  I am not sure how to get DECterm to
do this either.

I can't believe the functionality isn't there, but I just can not find
the place (I assume some X11 resource) to set it.  I have found this
most frustrating.  The preferable solution in my opinion would be to
fix the VMS terminal driver to be able to remap these two keys and thus
talk to a large number of existing machines much better.

Another workaround is to use "xterm -name VMSterm -e telnet vms".  Then
create translation resources that remap the BackSpace/Delete keys for
VMSterm.  The drawback of this method is that a) you don't get to use
the nifty DECterm; b) you tie up a tty on your system; c) you have to
type your login/password everytime you login to the VMS system; d) if
