	What you have on the back of a DECstation 2100 is what we call a 
DECconnect serial port. DECconnect is based on EIA RS-423 electrical 
spcifications. The RJ45 like connector is called an MMJ (Modified Modular Jack).
Notice that the locking tab is offset from center making it impossible to plug
in a standard modular telephone cord without cutting off the tab. There are 
four signals, using six wires, supported on "DEC-423" cabling, Transmit Data
(TXD+) and (TXD-GND), Receive Data, (RXD+) and (RXD-GND), Ready IN, and Ready
OUT. The Ready IN and OUT signal were designed for hardware flow control for
printers. These two signal are approxomately analogus to DTR and DSR. The are
a number of cables and adapters that would allow you to connect a modem to the
port on your DS 2100. The standard cable is BC16E-nn when nn is the cable
length in feet. There is an adapter from MMJ to a EIA-232 D-sub male, part
number, H8571-D you would use for connection to a modem.

	I would not recommend the connection of a modem to the DEC-423 serial
port on a DS 2100. With the minimal control signal support provided by 
DECconnect there is a large security hole opened by connecting a modem to
that port. It is possible to have a connection get dropped without the system
dectecting that the connection was lost. This can cause the system to fail to
kill the process that was connected to that port leaving the port logged in
when the next call to the modem is received. Outbound connections are less of
a problem but the same error can occur.

