!|> Well, no and yes. No ZLX graphics board has a function to convert
24-bit
!|> images to 8-bit dithered images.
!

  The ZLX-E* contains an implementation of a patented ordered dither
  that indeed should be capable of dithering a 24-bit image into a
  8-bit image.  I'm not sure that it has a pixel remap function, and
  I believe the resultant 8-bit image is in a 8-bit TrueColor format.

  I talked to the software half of the co-architect of the ZLX-E* a
  long time ago about how a "normal" user (i.e. not a OpenGL user)
  might access this to do high-performance 8-bit images from a 24-bit
  source, and suggested an extension that would allow a XImage
  operation that would have the server "do the right thing".  It
  sounded like a good idea (at least to me ;-), but it's just a
  "wouldn't it be nice if..." as opposed to being on anyones list
  of things to do.

  So for now, OpenGL is apparently the only way to get to the whiz
  bang stuff in the chip.

