> [3000/800S as multiuser servers]

Sadly, I cannot tell about 800S because we do not have such (I wouldn't
mind, though :-), but I can tell about the multiuser capability of
our _old_ 3000/400Ss.

192 MB real mem each, 576 MB swap each, they can handle quite high
number of very heavy interactive use:

The following is an approximate job mix during a normal afternoon:	

	- 600-700 processes, of then ~400 on ptys
	- ~120 users (about 100 unique users) --> ~120 tcshs, zshs
	- ~50 xterms
	- 20-40 GNU Emacs 19s doing GNUS, VM, irc
	- 20-30 rn readers, elms
	- 10 nn readers
	- 10-20 Mosaics
	- ircs, talks, ...


	- during the afternoon load normally about 30-60% of the swap is used.
	- about 10 of the users is logged in 24h a day (=staff).

During the high load normally about 30-60% of the swap is used.
The load stays below 5 all the time, below 3 most of the time.
Beware of spin looping news/mail readers, though, we have reaper
jobs zapping those off.  Lazy swap allocation is also a must for heavy
multi-user host: eager swap allocation just takes too much of swap,
as we used that initially, the machines could handle only about half
the processes they handle now.  Also, don't try to spare neither on
the real nor the virtual memory: first buying a fast machine and
then suffocating it isn't exactly a bright thing to do.

The OSF/1 also seems quite stabile: we had 1.3a running for three
months (>100 days), then we had to powerdown the machines because of
electrical rewiring.  With 2.1 we had initially crashing problems
(wrong type (SysV) ptys, BSD ptys worked much better) for about a week
but as the Digital engineer sorted that one out, the machines have
again stayed up fine.

++jhi;





