In article <34nbce$act@lll-winken.llnl.gov>, oberman@icaen.llnl.gov writes...
>Complain to your service person's supervisor. The NPG bus grant jumper is
>purely a hardware issue due to the idea at the time the Unibus was designed
>that DMA was too comples to be handled by a single board interface. Looks
>silly today, but in the days of 7404s and 7410s, it took a LOT of chips to
>implement DMA. If you ever saw an original DR11-B general purpose DMA
>interface, you will see what I mean! Four slots (I don't remember how many
>cards any more)! 

No do I, and my 3 DR11-B's are at home, but I remember a quad card, the
standard M7821 interrupt card (although not the M105 address logic - that was
on the quad card), and a lot of single-height flip-chip cards.

> 
>The jumper must be removed for any card that has DMA capability. Otherwise it
>will lock up on each DMA bus request as it will not receive the Non-Processor
>bus Grant (NPG).

In fact, what happens is that the card holds NPG out low (the grants are active
high), and as this is connected to NPG in, that is forced low also. So, when
the arbitor/CPU/previous DMA card releases the NPG in signal, it cannot go
high, as it is held low by NPG out. What a mess!!!.

> 
>I don't have any Unibus doc handy, but to the best of my recollection the
>jumper ia between AA1 and AB1 on a quad card. (But it may be AA2 and AB2.)

It's CA1-CB1. The convention all the manuals I have use is that the connectors
on a Unibus backplane used by a quad card are C,D,E,F. Some manuals call the
connectors a quad card C,D,E,F also.

>By looking at the backplane the jumper wires are usually pretty obvious as they
>are wired between the same to pins of each empty or non-DMA slot. On more
>recent backplanes they were yellow while mnost of the backplane wire was blue.

Be careful. There are a lot of wires between bins on the same slot - the BR/BG
signals from D to F, the address signals from E-C (or is it D), etc. The jumper
is between 2 adjacent pins on the same connector.

For some inexplicable reason, DEC put the jumpers on before all the other wire,
making it impossible to unwrap them, and making them difficult to cut. I always
refit them with red wire - they're then easy to spot.

> 
>R. Kevin Oberman			

-tony

