  I don't have any Unibus doc handy, but to the best of my recollection the
  jumper ia between AA1 and AB1 on a quad card. (But it may be AA2 and AB2.)

Nope, NPG is only on the top two pins of the C part of the module.

NPG needs to be out on cards taht can do Non Processor Request (this is
real DMA, the card talks to the memory without the processor involved
unlike this IBM-PC DMA crapola).  It needs to be jumpered in boards
that don't NPR.  It also needs to be in place for empty slots.  Now
as UNIBUS matured, some of the board makers started getting smart and
jumpering those fingers together on the board making it no longer required
to go put the jumper back.  You have to check the board if you want to
skip putting the jumper in (though having both doesn't hurt).

In addition to this, is the Bus Grant continuity that loops through each
card as well.  This is required for interrupts to work and hence all cards
maintain these (four) signals.  However, you need to stick the Bus Grant
Continuity cards into any empty slots to allow the signal to propagate
down to cards farther in the chain.  Again as UNIBUS matured, people got
smart and made the card wide enough to span both the BG signals in the
D connector and the NPG singal in the C, so if you stick one of these
cards in it takes card of the missing jumper as well.  In a brilliant
stroke of ergonomics the same people made these cards as long as the
rest of the boards and added a handle so you could put them in and take
them out without resorting to pulling the other adjacent boards.

Ahh, it brings back such glorius memories of loosing my skin on the
solder side of boards and poking my fingers on wire wrap pins,
playing origami with the UNIBUS cables, and machines you had to
boot "HALT START 777765000 LOADADDRESS RUN START" rather than "reset"...

-Ron

... and the revelation that the M that starts off the part numbers of
those boards stands for "Magenta" the color of the handle.


