  We have recently installed a 25.8 GB RAID-5 disk (fast SCSI interface,
[7 x 3.5" 4.3 GB disk]) on our DEC 3000-400 running Open VMS AXP V6.1.
In the VMS V6.0 and the later version, the maximum size of one disk
volume is available up to 1 TB under Files-11. (It was 8 GB before VMS
V6.0)  That's fine.  fine. 

  This 25.8 GB RAID-5 disk can be initialized, but the minimum cluster 
size we can specify is 50.   50  !!!

% (a part of "$ help init/cluster_size" )
%
%        /CLUSTER_SIZE=number-of-blocks
%
%     Defines, for disk volumes, the minimum allocation unit, in
%     blocks. The maximum size you can specify for a volume is one-
%     hundredth the size of the volume; the minimum size you can
%     specify is calculated with the following formula:
%
%                        disk_size(number_of_blocks)
%                        ---------------------------
%                                 255*4096

  This "cluster size = 50" makes a BIG problem for us. After migrating
all the user files from the previous RA90/92 disks (cluster size = 3) to
this newly installed RAID5 disk (cluster size = 50), the disk quota of
each user increased about twice or three times larger than before. 
Alas !   Every user in our VMS cluster claimed today as: 

  "Hello, System manager. Something is wrong with my disk quota. 
   It is now three times (or twice) larger than before. Oh my God ! "

  This is because the file allocation is based on the cluster size. On
the disk of a cluster size = 50, the file allocation size for a small
file (even one byte of its content) is 50 blocks (25 kB). Therefore the
disk quota for the users who have many small files (less then 50 blocks)
increased significantly  by the disk swapping to the large volume RAID
disk. 

  In order to make full use of a large capacity RAID disk on VMS, the
minimum cluster size we can specified should be smaller than the current
restrictions, I suppose.  We need at least, two or three more bits now. 

  Does anyone know any patch to fix this big cluster-size problems ? Is
there any plan to decrease the values of this minimum cluster size for a
large capacity disk (especially for RAID) in the future release of the
VMS ?   Thanks for any comment.  

