DIGITAL has a supported parititioning capability for OpenVMS, in the form of
the StorageWorks RAID Software for OpenVMS, which allows you to divide up a
RAID array or an individual disk into multiple partitions. Each partition
could then have its own clustersize based on its individual size.

The virtual disk driver VDdriver can also be used to accompish this.

In the mean time, I suppose you could cut the extend of the problem in 
half by opting for two or three smaller raid-5 sets instead of 1 large one.
The smaller sets can be joined back as a single bound volume to present
a single disk to the end users again.

>  Does anyone know any patch to fix this big cluster-size problems ? Is
>there any plan to decrease the values of this minimum cluster size for a
>large capacity disk (especially for RAID) in the future release of the
>VMS ?   Thanks for any comment.  

Sorry, this can NOT be patched. It is an inherent restriction of 
the ODS-II on disk file structure as maintained by the XQP.

To the best of my knowledge (I do NOT work in that area though) there
are no immediate plans to address this limit for ODS-II.
Digital is however working on a new file system (approaching field test 
now) which will address this and many other architectual restrictions.

