Our AXP application is a equation-based optimization system for continouse
processes in refineries, such as distilling units and catalytic cracking
units. It's written in FORTRAN-77, with some VMS dependencies, such as
system service and run-time library called. The first port was done shortly
prior to my hiring on here, but the person who did it said it was just
a matter of compiling and linking. 

We bought our ALPHA, a 3000/500 workstation, under one the the "50%-off 
ISV offers". As this was a giant leap from the two VAXes, VAX 3600 and 
VAXStation 3100/38, I'm not sure a cost comparison is very meaningful.
The 3000/500 is a desk-side unit with a 10" color monitor. DEC offered
server versions of their workstations, without the graphics monitor or
controller.

The ALPHA we bought came with 256 Mbytes of memory. When we needed to expand
the DEC sales rep said that DEC didn't have denser chips to go past 256 Mb
and that we should investigate 3rd-party memory vendors. We added another
256 Mbytes, but had to remove 64 Mbytes of the DEC memory to make room,
leaving the final total at 448 Mbytes. 

The system has been extremely reliable, and the only other additions have
been more disk space, in a StorageWorks enclosure. Since we run OpenVMS AXP,
the system management load is the same as a VAX. 

You might be interested in the 3000/900s, which are available as a workstation
or a rack-mounted server (there'll probably be a desk-side server later),
which can take up to 1 Gbyte of memory. That's roughly equivalent to a
