LinkWorks is an application integration framework.  It allows you to build
a
cohesive workgroup using almost all of your existing software packages.
Every LinkWorks user has customizable desktop that contains different
kinds of objects.  There are essentially three kinds of objects: data
objects,
tool objects, and container objects.  Data objects are such things like
text
such as a MS-Word document, a Quicktime movie, or a spreadsheet.  Tool
objects behave like applications, that is the may be launched, have file
dropped
into them for processing, and etc.  The third kind of object is a
container
object; this is analogous to folders, drawers, and file cabinets.

Objects are created with generic types such as text documents, notes, or
spreadsheets.  The generic class definition on a particular MS
Windows client for the text object might be setup to be WordPerfect for
Windows, but on a Macintosh client it might be MacWrite.  When users
share objects they pass pointers to the data objects to other users using
a
relational database as the engine.  
If I create a text object (in my case we'll say on
a windows client w/ MS-Word) and share it with another user it will appear
to them a text object. When they open it will launch what ever
application has been defined (yes there are defaults if you like) for
their
workstation (lets say a Mac running MacWrite).  The open sequence launches
MacWrite with the object as the data file.  Since these data types are
different
(Word vs. MacWrite)  MacWrite will ask "do you want to convert the Word
document..."

LinkWorks allows you to integrate your existing application in addition to
those already integrated with the product out of the box. I'd say the
paradigm
it was built on was " ... I don't care what you have you can still use
it!'
Customizations with the current product release are all done LinkWorks 
itself.  

LinkWorks supports MS-Windows, Macintosh, OS/2, and OSF/Motif clients. 
It currently requires TCP/IP or DECNET on the clients. There are a bunch
of 
server platforms and relational databases supported from both DEC and non-
DEC sources.

If you have more specific questions please feel free to contact me.
(There is a MS-Windows demo diskette available, but I strongly urge
getting
first hand experience either at a trade show or better still with a live
demo.  It 
is a difficult product to explain in words, but a simple thing to show in
person...)

