In article <vsocciCv4wwq.EJz@netcom.com> vsocci@netcom.com (Vance Socci) writes:

>    It is a matter of legend that Bob Clements' huge record
>    collection and his love for collecting pop records was responsible
>    for the TOPS-10 name. For years, there was a record collector's
>    database program in the DECUS library written by RCC . . .

     Well, it is the case that Clements wrote a sorting program to
     sort his record collection, and this program became the first
     sort utility available on the -10.  However, I don't recall any
     connection between that and the name TOPS-10.

     I'm pretty sure the name TOPS was used for the operating system
     from as early as the mid-60's when it was shipped on the PDP-6.
     It was derived from Timeshared OPerating System, but the name was
     mostly just used in DEC product references.

     Then the -20 came along, it became necessary to distingush the
     two operating systems, so TOPS-10 and TOPS-20 became commonly
     used.  Before that, the -10 operating system was generally
     referred to as "the monitor".


     dlm


