I'm currently using a 3000/300LX with OSF/1 v 2.1.

The system has 32 MB RAM, a _very_ noisy 1GB hard disk (RZ26L) and a
malfunctioning keyboard (LK401-AA, Rev C01 - the problem has already
been described here).

The OS is a memory pig:

ues5:~ 31> ps aux | egrep 'X11/X|PID'
USER       PID %CPU %MEM   VSZ  RSS TT  S    STARTED         TIME COMMAND
root      2317  2.0  6.5 37.6M 1.9M ??  S      Sep 01    58:47.99 /usr/bin/X11/X 
danpop   16286  1.0  0.9 1.30M 288K p7  S  + 17:12:27     0:00.05 egrep X11/X|PID

So, a plain egrep takes 1.3 MB of virtual memory and the X server 37.6
MB! Until a couple of weeks ago I was running the same X clients on a
(non-DEC) X terminal with only 8 MB of RAM.

This means a lot of paging for each command, which implies a lot of
noise (remember the RZ26L) and very poor interactive response. 

Is OSF/1 v 3.0 supposed to improve the memory usage in a significant
way or am I better off turning the machine into an X terminal connected
to the PowerPC which served me well when all I had on my desk was an
X terminal?

Another question:

ues5:~ 35> vmstat
Virtual Memory Statistics: (pagesize = 8192)
  procs    memory         pages                          intr        cpu      
  r  w  u  act  free wire fault cow zero react pin pout  in  sy  cs  us  sy  id
  2 74 35  1502  296 1509   1M 287K 535K 143K 526K  55K  43 -11019  83  17  33  50

How am I supposed to interpret -11019 system calls per second?

And a final question. When we tried to install the AFS client software
on OSF/1 1.3, the kernel building procedure bombed out because the 
AdvFS code redefined the strcat() standard library function. DEC sent us
a patch to fix the problem. A couple of days ago, we received the AFS
software for OSF/1 v 2.x and we tried to install it. The AdvFS strcat()
bug was still present, despite the fact that DEC knows about the problem.
So, we had to remove the AdvFS from the kernel to install AFS. Will 3.0
finally fix this annoying bug? The place of strcat() is in the standard
C library, not in the Advanced File System code!

Dan
--
