Sorry Terry, but wrong again. The Department of Defense is one US government 
agency that 's using OSI.

Also, several of the Fortune 1000 are converting. Want me to name a couple? 
OK. How about MCI, Sprint, NASDAQ?

As to the reply from Ken at TGV at even DEC doesn't use it...WRONG. I've 
spoken to several Digital employes that tell me that DEC either has converted 
sites to OSI or is in the process of converting. Why so long? Over 100,000 
nodes around the world. By the end of this year, most DEC sites, all around 
the world, will be using OSI.

Before you move your applications to TCP/IP, take a look at where DEC is 
going. Version 6.0 of Decnet/OSI is in field test. This version includes an IP 
stack. 

Compare the features of Phase/IP to Decnet/OSI 6.0. Call the program manager 
for Decnet/OSI - Judy Cross - she'll be happy to tell you what Decnet/OSI will 
do and what Phase/IP WON'T do.
For instance, DEC's implementation will run ANY vendors IP. Multinet's will 
run only Multinet. DEC doesn't require a new fee, ask Multinet how much it's 
going to cost you to upgrade to Phase/IP.

And then there's support. Multinet- ZERO! I've been there.

Am I saying you should immediately upgrade to Decnet/OSI? No. Some sites don't 
need the capabilities of Decnet/OSI. But, before you decide to abandon the 
idea for another vendors product, take a closer look.


As I've said before: I've been involved with Decnet/OSI from the beginning.  I 
even teach Decnet/OSI(My own company - THIS IS NOT AN AD). I've been really 
busy lately teaching just that one product. Even though there were problems in 
the beginning, DEC has done everything to accomodate it's user community. 

Expect a lot from Digital. Don't count them out yet. 




