In article <346l15INNba1@usenet.pa.dec.com>, neideck@nestvx.enet.dec.com (Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz) writes:
|> In article <3455ab$pe7@ztivax.zfe.siemens.de> beck@dali11.zfe.siemens.de (Andre Beck) writes:
|> >|> Well, the problem is that free-ing memory does *not* return the memory
|> >|> to the system under most versions of UNIX.
|> >
|> >However, one would expect that OSF/1 would deal with it better. Of course
|> >it does, in version 1.3 at least. For some strange reasons, DEC OSF/1 2.0
|> >is _still_ not 1.3, but 1.2 ;(
|> 
|> DEC OSF/1 V3.0's malloc *does* give memory back when the conditions are
|> right. And we didn't pick that feature up from OSFs OSF/1. DEC OSF/1 cannot
|> be characterized as being any one of the different OSF OSF/1 releases,
|> it contains technology from several and then a lot more.
|> 

Is 3.0 out yet ? I accept that DEC OSF/1 is not exactly the same as
OSF/1 (vendor-adaption was one of the goals of OSF). However, mentioning
OSF/1 1.3 vs. 1.2 I mainly pointed to the difference between a Mach 2.5
Quasi-BSD Kernel vs. a Mach 3.0 real microkernel with OSF beeing a server
on it. This difference lets 1.3 introduce the concept of sparse filled
address spaces, which is the only _real_ way to allow free() to give
memory back to the system. The probability that one can lower the break
on a random free() is not that high - even with sorted pools. Thats a
problem which is immanent to the linear-extended address space concept.

