In Article <1994Sep8.003217.286@relay.nswc.navy.mil>
Don Pugsley <dpugsle@nswc-wo.nswc.navy.mil> writes:

>I remember a wire-wrap jumper on the backplane that needs to be installed 
>or removed for a DMA device (DRE-11 was what I used before) but I don't 
>remember the details...  can anyone help? Is there a technical contact 
>number at DEC for stuff at this level? 
>
>Our service *(&*^$&^$ person (Charleston, S.C) won't do anything besides 
>replace the board; he says the system contract covers hardware only, and 
>the DMA jumper is a driver configuration software problem.  I used to
>think 
>a DEC service contract was worth something...

Complain to your service person's supervisor. The NPG bus grant jumper is
purely a hardware issue due to the idea at the time the Unibus was designed
that DMA was too comples to be handled by a single board interface. Looks
silly today, but in the days of 7404s and 7410s, it took a LOT of chips to
implement DMA. If you ever saw an original DR11-B general purpose DMA
interface, you will see what I mean! Four slots (I don't remember how many
cards any more)! 

The jumper must be removed for any card that has DMA capability. Otherwise it
will lock up on each DMA bus request as it will not receive the Non-Processor
bus Grant (NPG).

I don't have any Unibus doc handy, but to the best of my recollection the
jumper ia between AA1 and AB1 on a quad card. (But it may be AA2 and AB2.)

If you have a HEX card, it's CA1 and CB1 (or CA2 and CB2). On the backplane
it's at the top of the third "group" of connectors, the first past the rows
into which the Unibus itself connects.

By looking at the backplane the jumper wires are usually pretty obvious as they
are wired between the same to pins of each empty or non-DMA slot. On more
recent backplanes they were yellow while mnost of the backplane wire was blue.

