In article <34l3h1$ql0@charm.magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>,
Morton E O'Kelly <mokelly@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu> wrote:
>Please help a beginner! I have a dec alpha 3000/600 which runs into
>warning messages about swap space below 10%. When this happens df
>says:
>
>Filesystem   512-blocks        Used       Avail Capacity  Mounted on
>/dev/rz3a        126462      105500        8314    93%    /
>/dev/rz3g       1603526      868410      574762    60%    /usr
>
>so I appear to have a lot of space on /dev/rz3g.  How do I tell the
>machine to use that as swap space? I am the only user of the system, and
>I can allocate all resources to solving the problem.
>My e-mail address is okelly+@osu.edu

yep... this is not an easy thing to do for a beginner.

step by step...

1)	find an unused partition on your disk. To do this you need to use the
	disklabel command (read the man page).
	ex. csh> disklabel -r rz3
	If there is a small unused partition on your disk (i.e. the cyl. do not
	overlap with a used partition) then go on to 4

2)	backup everything

3)	use 'disklabel -er rz3' to edit the disklabel and make a new, small
	partition. You have to calculate the parameters and everything, so it 
	is not easy. READ AND UNDERSTAND THE MAN PAGE.

4)	use newfs to create filesystems on the new partition(s).

5)	edit your /etc/fstab to show the new configuration.

6)	reboot

I think that should work. If you have problems, or don't understand something,
write me and I will try to help more.

Thomas Wright


