In article <34qsag$s9@larch.cc.swarthmore.edu>, he@engin.swarthmore.edu 
(Ye He) writes:
>I am a novice user of DEC.  I was trying to compile a fortran program
>that compiled and ran on Apollos (Domain OS) and DECfort77  gave me
>the following error message:
>
>ld:
>Undefined variable:
>max_
>fort:Sever:Failed while trying to link.
>
>I am not sure what went wrong.
>
>The program declared a lot of "double precision" variables.  If my DEC3000
>(running Alpha) uses 64 bits by default, then doubling  may get me in 
>trouble. If that is the reason, how can I turn the double precision mode
>off in DECfort77?  I couldn't find it in the man page.  

It is a common misconception that because the AXP is a 64-bit processor
that integers and floats automatically default to 64 bits.  Not true.
DOUBLE PRECISION is a 64-bit type the same as it is on most other
systems.  You don't need to turn anything off.

I'm a bit confused by the error message - normally, if you used the
MAX intrinsic function the compiler would automatically map it to
the correct library routine (which isn't max_), but the fact that you
got a reference to max_ shows that something else is wrong.  Is there
an EXTERNAL MAX somewhere in your program?  How is the identifier MAX
used?  Feel free to send me mail with code samples and I'll try to help.

