that. However Borland C++ 3.1 has the option "Fast floating point", its help
says "When this option is off, the compiler follows strict ANSI rules regarding
floating-point conversion". After turned off this option, I got the exact same
results with VAX on PC.

Example :

#define PI  3.14159265358979323846264338327950288

int main()
{
	int i = 100;
	double b;

	b = cos((float)(PI * 0.5));
	printf("%20.10lf\n", b);

	b = cos((float)(2.0 * PI * i / 400));
	printf("%20.10lf\n", b);
}

VAX, BC(FFP off) print
       -0.0000000437
       -0.0000000437

BC(FFP on), VC(emu, inline) print
       -0.0000000437
        0.0000000000

VC(80x87, co-pro) print
        0.0000000000
       -0.0000000000

