In article <1994Aug31.095400.1@slacvx.slac.stanford.edu>, 
fairfield@slacvx.slac.stanford.edu writes:
|>In article <CvE5Hn.ApA@world.std.com>, 
|>    			jg1ifj@world.std.com (Hisa Noguchi) writes:
|>> Does anyone know of the any VAX floating point emulation library for non-VAX
|>> system (esp. for PC).
|>> 
|>> I'm porting a VAX program to PC which using a single precision floating point
|>> in many places. Because of the FPU differences, VAX and PC give different
|>> rounding error. It is not negligible for the single precision for this app.
|>> Unfortunately I can't modify VAX program (out of my control) to use double
|>> precision to reduce the differences.
|>
|>        Since a VAX F_float  (single  precision  floating point) has the
|>    same  precision  as an IEEE Single (used by Intel  processors),  and
|>    since the VAX and IEEE singles have _almost_  the  same  range,  you
|>    shouldn't  (in  principle) have to do anything.  However, the Intel,
|>    especially if you have a  math  co-processor, is likely to keep more
|>    precision  in  the intermediate results, and I would expect that  is
|>    the difference you are seeing.  I believe there is a way to get your
|>    compiler (you didn't  say  which  language)  to  truncate  or  round
|>    intermediate   results   back  to  single  precision.   That  should
|>    reproduce the VAX results...

It's not that simple.  VAX and IEEE FP round differently when the infinite
precision result is exactly halfway between two representable values. VAX
will round "away from zero" (positive numbers round up, negative numbers
round down), but the default IEEE mode is "round to even" which causes it
to pick whichever representable value has a LSB of zero.  I have seen an
application where this difference in a single operation created wildly
different final results.  Of course, the application itself was written with
a complete lack of understanding of floating point weaknesses and the need
to maintain extra precision (not to mention that you should avoid rounding
results and feeding those back into future iterations).

I can believe that Hisa's application is dependent on VAX rounding to obtain
the "desired" results.  Unfortunately, I have no idea what can be done
to mimic the VAX behavior on the Intel system.  I would think that, unless
the application has made the same mistakes as the one I mentioned above, that
the differences should be minor.   An important factor, not mentioned so far,
is the quality of the math library, if used.  On VAX we take pride in the
accurate results of the math library, and have spent many years making sure
they're "good to the last bit".  Some vendors don't take quite so much care.
-- 

