In article <GOEL.94Aug31151111@tune.cs.columbia.edu>, goel@tune.cs.columbia.edu
(Shantanu Goel) writes:

|>1) My University plans to junk a MicroVAX II.  I'm thinking of taking the
|>beast home.  Does it require any special power circuitry or I can just
|>plug it into the wall like (say) an IBM PC?

  It plugs into a standard 15A socket.  (The BA23 MicroVAX II is rated at
  6 amps at 100-120 volts.)

|>2) Around what time did this machine come out?  I know it is rated at
|>about 0.9 MIPS which makes it slower than a VAX 11/780.  At what speed
|>does the bus (Qbus?) run on this machine?

   Around 1986.

|>3) I'm a fan of CISC processors and the VAX has certainly got to be the
|>most CISCy of them all. 

   To quote one of the locals, "What's your point?"

|>Reading old BSD VAX dependant code, it seems
|>the MicroVAX II does not support all of the instructions in the original
|>(classic?) VAX 11/780.

   The VAX architecture was subsetted, and those groups of instructions
   that were not present in hardware were emulated.  The VAX Architecture
   Reference Manual (see below) covers this topic in detail.

|>            Never (alas) having had experience programming VAXen, my only
|>knowledge of them comes from the old source code for BSD.  I'm curious to
|>know much the current models differ from the originals like the 11/780,
|>11/750, 8600 etc. 

    There are very few (or no) architectural differences, though the
    newer models are vastly different in terms of I/O, performance,
    size and implementation technologies.

|>Do any present models support Unibus and Massbus adaptors? 

    Newer processors generally do not directly support the Massbus
    or Unibus.  (One may be able to find a bus widget that allows
    one to connect a more modern processor to them, but unless there
    a very special and correspondingly ancient widget, there is
    usually very little point in doing so.)

|>How much of the original instruction set has been preserved?

    Avoiding discussions of the "original" instructions in the VAX
    architecture and of the particular instructions and instruction
    groups added to the VAX Architecture, all supported VAX processors
    implement or emulate all instructions in the architecture.  The
    MicroVAX II is a supported processor. For details, see the VAX
    Architecture Reference (see below), and the Software Product
    Description (SPD) for the desired operating system(s).

|>4) Does DEC still print the VAX Hardware Handbook?  If not, is there a
|>replacement for it (I'd appreciate an ISBN number)?  Basically, I would
|>like to get hold of a processor manual similar to what one can get for
|>an 80386 or 68030 from their respective manufacturers.

    The VAX Architecture Reference Manual (2nd ed) is EY-F576E-DP. 
    The manual specific to the KA630 processor used in the MicroVAX
    II is the EK-KA630-UG.  The ISBN on the former is 1-55558-057-2.
    I do not have an ISBN listing for the latter.

    The VAX Hardware Handbook provides a high-level overview of the
    various models in the VAX family, and is generally useful for
    determining implementations, specifications and configurations.
    The KA630 document is better suited to folks that are implementing
    extremely low-level code and/or operating systems on the KA630.

    My personal preference for a home system -- if I were in the
    market -- would be a used VAXstation from the 3100 or the 4000
    series.  They're cheap, considerably faster than the MicroVAX II,
    have native networking and graphics, and they use SCSI storage...

