All of this depends on what you are trying to DO.  If you are
saying that you are going to chip level troubleshoot the thing,
then the 40 is surely easier than a 23 from a "getting parts"
perspective, though it *might* take a lot less time to figure 
out which chip to replace on a 23, since there are only a few 
of them.  This is where having access to the internal data paths
on the machine is valuable - I have trouble shot a KA-10 by 
swapping JK Flip Flops (DEC Flip Chip R series logic) around,
putting the marginal ones into areas where they were not 
required to flip and flop quite so fast...

If you are talking BOARD level, then no contest.  Swap the 
23 out and be done with it.  The whole system will be a few
boards (memory, CPU, BVD or other terminator, maybe a J board
for IO, disk?) 

So, it depends on what you mean "easier to work on" or "easier
to fix".  On a desert island, with just a 40 and a small freighter
filled with TTL, you are golden.  In your living room, with all the
time in the world and enjoyin the hunt for the hardware bug, again
the 40 is your machine of choice.  In an operation where downtime
counts and people thing that a soldering iron is a coffee warmer,
the 23 makes more sense, especially since I have seen them for 
$25 each (hell, they were even 23+ units ;-).

